  "id": "COMPOUND:36006 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Taxinin M is shown to interact with Casein kinase II subunit beta, which in turn targets DNA polymerase iota through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Taxinin M may inhibit DNA polymerase iota by modulating the activity of Casein kinase II subunit beta, which is a key regulator of DNA replication and repair. This mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple targets that are downstream of Casein kinase II subunit beta, including compounds like (4aS,6aR,6aS,6bR,8aR,9R,12aR,14bS)-9-(hexadecanoyloxymethyl)-2,2,6a,6b,9,12a-hexamethyl-10-oxo-3,4,5,6,6a,7,8,8a,11,12,13,14b-dodecahydro-1H-picene-4a-carboxylic acid and GSK-376501, both of which are known to interact with DNA polymerase iota. The consistent interaction between Taxinin M and Casein kinase II subunit beta, along with the direct targeting of DNA polymerase iota, suggests a plausible mechanism of action involving the regulation of DNA polymerase activity. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Taxinin M and DNA polymerase iota, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the specific role of Casein kinase II subunit beta in this process.",
